[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: ZEF WHAT — system behavior contract
UID: ZEF-WHAT-DOC
VERSION: 1.0.0
DATE: 2026-10-10
PREFIX: ZEF-WHAT
ROOT: False
OPTIONS:
  ENABLE_MID: False
  MARKUP: Text

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
WHAT items are the formal contract for the ZEF system boundary. Every item is a
normative shall statement. WHAT defines observable behavior and limits without
choosing data structures, function names, or algorithms. INTERFACE items describe
the surface of this contract; IMPLEMENTATION items select its concrete solution.
<<<

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Boundary, configuration, and trust

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ZEF-WHAT-001
TITLE: Embedded framework boundary
STATEMENT: ZEF shall provide an independent event-driven framework for small microcontrollers, including ARM Cortex-M0 and Cortex-M0+ targets, without depending on a general-purpose operating system.
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ZEF-WHY-001

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ZEF-WHAT-002
TITLE: Predictable build-time storage
STATEMENT: >>>
ZEF shall make framework RAM requirements predictable at build time. For a
configuration and caller-supplied storage, the required framework storage shall
be calculable from configured limits, public object sizes, and documented
caller-storage declarations. ZEF shall not use a general-purpose runtime
allocator. Execution-time bounds are specified only where an individual
requirement states them.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ZEF-WHY-002

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ZEF-WHAT-003
TITLE: Configuration limits
STATEMENT: >>>
ZEF shall expose configurable limits for registered reactors,
topics, message pools, state depth, queue capacity, tick width, and ownership
counters. The public configuration header shall define the valid range for
each limit and shall reject invalid values at build time, before runtime use.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ZEF-WHY-002

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ZEF-WHAT-004
TITLE: Requirement traceability
STATEMENT: >>>
ZEF shall define public concepts, object responsibilities,
state-machine behavior, ownership behavior, scheduler behavior, and port
behavior in traceable documents.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ZEF-WHY-003

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ZEF-WHAT-007
TITLE: Consistent public vocabulary
STATEMENT: >>>
ZEF shall use one documented vocabulary for its public concepts
across requirements, interfaces, examples, and verification artifacts. The
vocabulary record shall define the spelling and meaning of each public term,
and the review shall report any term used outside that record.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ZEF-WHY-010

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ZEF-WHAT-005
TITLE: Distribution terms
STATEMENT: >>>
The ZEF framework shall be licensed under the Apache 2.0 license.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ZEF-WHY-031

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ZEF-WHAT-006
TITLE: Fatal-condition boundary
STATEMENT: >>>
Fatal resource, ownership, configuration, argument, lifecycle, and
state violations shall invoke the application panic handler, shall not be
represented as ordinary recoverable results, and shall not return. The
interface shall assign each fatal condition to a documented panic reason;
ordinary results shall be limited to documented expected observations.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ZEF-WHY-002

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Message behavior

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ZEF-WHAT-010
TITLE: Message identity and typed payload
STATEMENT: >>>
ZEF shall provide a public Message structure with an application-visible topic
identity and a payload size, and shall support fixed-size application-defined
payloads. ZEF shall also provide a caller-owned static Message form whose
payload is immutable after initialization and whose static kind is observable
through the public Message operations. Framework-owned allocation and ownership
fields shall be reserved by documentation and shall be accessed only through
the public Message operations.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ZEF-WHY-011

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ZEF-WHAT-011
TITLE: Message lifecycle and ownership
STATEMENT: >>>
A dynamic Message shall begin with one creator reference and be reclaimed only
after all references are released. A Message is writable through its mutable
construction pointer before transfer; framework-delivered Messages are read
through borrowed const pointers and may be shared by retaining references. A static Message shall be
initialized once, remain immutable for its caller-defined lifetime, and shall
not be associated with a Message pool or ownership claim. The framework shall
identify static Messages before applying dynamic claim or pool-reclamation
rules.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ZEF-WHY-011

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ZEF-WHAT-012
TITLE: Delivery ownership
STATEMENT: >>>
A successful normal delivery shall transfer one reference to its destination,
and a failed delivery shall create no destination reference or queued entry. A
``postpone`` delivery shall add one reference, and dequeue shall transfer the
destination reference to the receiver. Publication shall create one reference
per successful recipient without copying Message storage; publication to zero
recipients shall consume the creator reference and return a zero delivery
count. Static delivery shall enqueue a reference without changing claim
counts; the same static Message may be delivered repeatedly and to multiple
destinations. Static delivery shall not copy the payload, and queued static
references shall remain valid until the caller-defined static Message lifetime
ends.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ZEF-WHY-011

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ZEF-WHAT-013
TITLE: Message failure behavior
STATEMENT: >>>
Pool exhaustion, full-destination delivery, dynamic reference-count overflow,
release of a dynamic Message without a reference, invalid Message
arguments, and invalid Message lifecycle transitions shall invoke the
fatal-condition handler without silently ignoring error conditions. Retain and
release operations on a static Message shall be no-ops and shall not invoke the
fatal-condition handler. Before a fatal enqueue, the destination queue and
claim counts shall remain unchanged.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ZEF-WHY-002

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Machine behavior

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ZEF-WHAT-020
TITLE: Hierarchical state behavior
STATEMENT: >>>
A Machine shall support nested State nodes, parent-directed message
handling, explicit handled or unhandled outcomes, transition requests, entry
and exit behavior, and no allocation during dispatch. If every visited handler
returns unhandled, dispatch shall complete without changing the current State
or invoking entry or leave hooks.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ZEF-WHY-012

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ZEF-WHAT-021
TITLE: Deterministic transitions
STATEMENT: >>>
Machine transitions shall leave source states in reverse hierarchy order,
excluding the common ancestor. They shall then enter target states in
hierarchy order, following declared initial children. For a self-transition,
the machine shall execute the leave handler followed by the enter handler for
the current state before following its declared initial children.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ZEF-WHY-012

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ZEF-WHAT-022
TITLE: Machine startup and composition
STATEMENT: >>>
Machine startup shall enter the root and its initial-child chain exactly once;
dispatch before startup and repeated startup shall be fatal. A Reactor shall
support application code dispatching one read-only Message synchronously to a
primary Machine and to any auxiliary Machines, including submachines, that the
application creates and manages. The framework shall not create, register,
schedule, or remove those auxiliary Machines; their lifetime, storage, and
composition remain application responsibilities.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ZEF-WHY-012

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ZEF-WHAT-023
TITLE: State graph validity
STATEMENT: >>>
A Machine shall validate its State graph during initialization when assertion
checking is enabled. A malformed graph has a null root, a non-null root parent,
a non-root node with a null parent, a parent chain that does not reach the
root, a cycle in parent or initial links, an initial child whose parent is not
the containing node, or a depth greater than the configured state-depth limit.
The root shall be non-null and have no parent; a null initial link denotes a
leaf. A transition target shall be non-null, have a parent chain that reaches
the root, and remain within the configured depth. In assertion-disabled builds
the application shall provide a graph with these properties; validation may be
omitted and behavior for a malformed graph is outside the contract.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ZEF-WHY-002

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Reactors, Mailboxes, and Kernel

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ZEF-WHAT-030
TITLE: Reactor boundary and lifecycle
STATEMENT: >>>
A Reactor shall reference one primary Machine, a bounded Mailbox,
an application context, and a scheduler identity. Registration shall move a
constructed Reactor to registered and start shall move it to running. A
registered Reactor remains in the Kernel registry for the Kernel lifetime;
Phase 1 provides no stop or remove operation. A registered identity is not a
stopped state, and posting to an unregistered identity is an invalid argument.
Auxiliary Machines, including submachines, are application-owned and are not
part of the framework-managed Reactor lifecycle.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ZEF-WHY-013

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ZEF-WHAT-031
TITLE: Mailbox behavior
STATEMENT: >>>
A Mailbox shall provide bounded FIFO delivery, optional front delivery,
dequeue, occupancy and free-space observations, selective removal, and claim
release for every removed dynamic Message. A front delivery is observed before
all previously queued entries; repeated front deliveries are observed in
last-in-first-out order. Selective removal shall evaluate its predicate once
per queued entry in delivery order and shall preserve the relative order of
entries that remain.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ZEF-WHY-020

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ZEF-WHAT-032
TITLE: No dynamic Reactor stop or removal
STATEMENT: >>>
Phase 1 shall not provide operations that stop or remove a Reactor. A Reactor
registered with a Kernel remains registered until the Kernel lifetime ends.
There is no stopped or removed Reactor state and no expected result for a
stop-or-remove operation. Delivery to an unregistered or invalid Reactor
identity is an invalid argument and invokes the panic handler.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ZEF-WHY-013

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ZEF-WHAT-033
TITLE: Application-owned deferred Mailbox
STATEMENT: >>>
An application shall be able to initialize a caller-owned Mailbox that is not
assigned to a Reactor and use it as a local deferred queue. The local queue
shall have the same bounded ordering, ownership, and removal semantics as a
Reactor Mailbox, but enqueueing or dequeuing it shall not change a Kernel
runnable identity or cause automatic dispatch. Application code shall choose
when and in which Machine state to drain it.

When a handler receives a borrowed dynamic Message that must be deferred,
``postpone`` to the local Mailbox shall retain one claim for the local queue
without copying the Message storage. For a static Message, it shall enqueue a
reference without changing claim state. Taking a dynamic entry later shall
transfer the local queue claim to the application, which shall release it
after processing or retain it before handing it to another owner. Taking a
static entry transfers no claim, and the same release operation remains valid
as a no-op. A full local queue is the same fatal enqueue condition as a full
Reactor Mailbox.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ZEF-WHY-014
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ZEF-WHY-020

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ZEF-WHAT-040
TITLE: Cooperative scheduling
STATEMENT: >>>
The cooperative Kernel shall execute at most one complete Message dispatch per
finite scheduling step. A dispatch consists of taking one Message, invoking
    the selected Reactor's primary Machine dispatch and all handler, entry, leave,
    and transition callbacks caused by that Message, releasing or transferring
    any dynamic dispatch claim as applicable, and updating runnable state. The
    Kernel shall run handlers
to completion without blocking, shall
select the highest-index runnable Reactor, where the zero-based Reactor identity
is also its strict priority. It shall provide an idle path when no Reactor is
runnable.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ZEF-WHY-020

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ZEF-WHAT-041
TITLE: Runnable identity invariant
STATEMENT: >>>
The Kernel shall use explicit zero-based Reactor identities, with identity also
defining strict priority. Registered identities may have gaps. A registered,
running Reactor shall be runnable exactly when its Mailbox is nonempty; the
Kernel shall clear its runnable identity when the Mailbox becomes empty and
shall never dispatch an empty Mailbox. A registered Reactor that has not been
started is not runnable.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ZEF-WHY-020

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Topics, time, and ports

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ZEF-WHAT-050
TITLE: Topic publication
STATEMENT: >>>
The topic service shall maintain subscriptions, make subscription changes safe
against concurrent publication, snapshot recipients, consume the sender pointer before
snapshotting, and deliver recipients in descending zero-based Reactor identity
order, which is also descending strict priority order. A full recipient
shall be fatal; deliveries completed before the fatal condition are not rolled
back. Subscription changes and publication snapshots shall be linearized by
the Port critical section: a change completed after the snapshot does not
affect that publication, and a change completed before it does.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ZEF-WHY-021

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ZEF-WHAT-060
TITLE: Clock and Timer behavior
STATEMENT: >>>
A Timer shall have disarmed, armed, and expired/disarmed states;
the Clock service shall support one-shot and periodic timers, enqueue expiry
Messages rather than invoke application handlers from tick context, and shall
enqueue expiry Messages that are due on the same tick in their arm order. A period of
zero shall mean one-shot delivery. A delay of zero shall expire on the next
Clock tick. A periodic timer expiring at tick T shall be reinserted for T plus
its period using unsigned modular tick arithmetic, and a timer armed during
processing of tick T shall not expire until a later tick. Valid delays and
periods shall be less than half the representable tick range so expiration
ordering remains unambiguous across wraparound.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ZEF-WHY-022

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ZEF-WHAT-061
TITLE: Timer lifecycle errors
STATEMENT: >>>
Starting an armed Timer shall be fatal, stopping an already
disarmed Timer shall be an explicit expected observation, and cancellation
shall stop the Timer and remove that Timer's pending private Message deliveries
without removing unrelated deliveries. Cancellation of a Timer that
is neither armed nor pending shall return the same expected not-running
observation as stopping an already disarmed Timer when it has no retained
dynamic Message claim. Stopping a Timer shall preserve its configured Message
and retained base claim so the Timer can be armed again; cancellation is the
terminal operation that releases that retained claim.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ZEF-WHY-022

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ZEF-WHAT-070
TITLE: Port contract
STATEMENT: >>>
The Port shall provide interrupt-state entry and restoration, reschedule
request, and idle operations; nested critical sections shall restore the prior
state. The application shall provide one non-returning panic function, separate
from the Port, and all fatal framework conditions shall call that function.
The public API shall classify interrupt-context legality, with fixed-pool
Message construction and the documented Phase 1 posting and publication
operations supported from ISR context.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ZEF-WHY-023

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ZEF-WHAT-071
TITLE: Optional preemptive execution
STATEMENT: >>>
A Cortex-M0 preemptive port, when included in a phase baseline,
shall preserve the cooperative run-to-completion contract while allowing
deferred priority-based context switching and shall define stack, interrupt,
and safe termination behavior.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ZEF-WHY-024

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Verification and traceability

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ZEF-WHAT-080
TITLE: Public verification boundary
STATEMENT: >>>
Host verification shall exercise the public headers and linked
library, and shall cover ownership, pools, Mailboxes, Machines, topics, Clock
service, cooperative scheduling, interrupt masking, idle behavior,
and any preemptive scheduling model included in the host contract. Target-only
preemptive context switching is outside host verification unless explicitly
included in a phase baseline.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ZEF-WHY-030

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ZEF-WHAT-081
TITLE: Verification matrix
STATEMENT: >>>
Verification shall cover reactor-slot counts of 8, 16, and 32;
topic bitmap cases at one below, exactly at, and one above each bitmap-word
boundary that is strictly inside the configured topic range, while testing
only in-range values at the range endpoints; queue capacities of 1, 2, 3, and
the configured maximum for both Reactor and local deferred Mailboxes;
``postpone``, take, release, and deferred processing; one-shot
and periodic timers; one, two, and three Message pools; assertions enabled
and disabled; and AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer host
builds.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ZEF-WHY-030

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ZEF-WHAT-082
TITLE: Traceability completeness
STATEMENT: >>>
Every implementation and verification artifact shall link to the
WHAT, INTERFACE, or IMPLEMENTATION work item it satisfies, every non-root work
item shall have a parent, and every work item named in a phase baseline shall
have a downstream artifact or a deferral record naming the reason and target
phase.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ZEF-WHY-030

[[/SECTION]]
